  { id:'opmcq-c11-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 62-year-old diabetic man presents with progressive difficulty navigating in dim light and frequent tripping over objects. He reports no pain or redness. Examination shows IOP of 28 mmHg in both eyes, an increased cup-to-disc ratio of 0.8, and arcuate scotoma on automated perimetry. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Normal-tension glaucoma","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Retinal vein occlusion"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem hands over the whole triad, and the triad is the definition.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 52-58 print glaucoma as \"a triad of **Elevated intraocular pressure (IOP)** · **Optic nerve affection (cupping)** · **Visual field defects**\". This patient has all three: 28 mmHg against a normal range of **10-21 mmHg** (`L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` line 225, \"Normal range 10-21 mmHg (mean = 16 mmHg)\"; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.31 gives the same range), a **cup-to-disc ratio of 0.8** against `L11` line 137's threshold of \"enlargement of cup/disc ratio > 0.3\", and an **arcuate scotoma**, which `L11` line 151 lists among the field changes of glaucomatous rim thinning.\n\n**The two negatives in the stem do as much work as the three positives.** `L11` lines 181-186 give the presentation: \"Most commonly → **asymptomatic** · Less commonly → headache (early morning), frequent change of reading glasses · **Advanced disease → peripheral visual field loss, diminution of vision**\". A white, quiet, painless eye with peripheral field loss is the printed picture. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` puts it more bluntly still: \"The great majority of patients **lack pain, ocular inflammation**\" and \"much peripheral vision can be lost before the patient notices visual impairment.\" The tripping and the trouble in dim light are that peripheral loss described from the patient's side.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Normal-tension glaucoma** | ⚠️ **The one option that fails on a number rather than on a mechanism.** `L11` line 69 defines it as \"eyes with optic neuropathy and **normal IOP**\" — it is listed as an *exception* to the triad, alongside ocular hypertension (normal nerve, high IOP). At 28 mmHg this eye is not normotensive, so the exception does not apply. `opmcq-c11-12`, eleven questions later, is the same bank's version of this question with the pressure set to 18 |\n| **B. Chronic open-angle glaucoma** | The key. Elevated IOP + cupping + field defect, in a painless white eye |\n| **C. Optic neuritis** | Wrong on tempo and on the field. `L21) Optic Nerve` teaches it as an acute disease of a **swollen or normal** disc, not an excavated one; the cup is the abnormality here and `L21` slide 10 makes that the whole difference — \"the disc outline itself is sharp; it is the **CUP**, not the margin, that is abnormal. This is the difference from every swollen disc above.\" Optic neuritis also hurts on eye movement and drops central acuity early, neither of which is offered |\n| **D. Retinal vein occlusion** | A sudden, usually unilateral loss with a fundus full of haemorrhages, not a bilateral pressure of 28 with symmetrical cupping. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` teaches CRVO by its retinal signs. ⚠️ It is not an idle distractor in a diabetic — vein occlusion and proliferative diabetic retinopathy both cause **neovascular** glaucoma, which is a secondary angle closure (`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 290-304) — but that eye is red and painful, and this one is neither |\n\n*The diabetes in the stem is a red herring for this question and a live clue for two others in the chapter: `opmcq-c11-10` and `opmcq-c11-17` both turn on what a diabetic may and may not be given.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Elevated IOP, a cup-disc ratio well past 0.3 and an arcuate scotoma in a painless white eye are the printed triad of chronic open-angle glaucoma; normal-tension glaucoma is the same nerve and the same field with a NORMAL pressure.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.74 (Ch.11 Q1, the first question of the chapter; its key prints as "Answer: B." with a trailing full stop, the only one so punctuated in the chapter)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 64-year-old man with no eye pain presents with gradually reduced peripheral vision. IOP is 26 mmHg. Fundus exam shows cupping of the optic disc with a focal thinning of the neuroretinal rim between 6 and 7 o'clock. Visual field testing shows a corresponding superior arcuate scotoma. What is the most likely finding?",
    options:["Temporal pallor","Inferotemporal notching","Peripapillary atrophy","Optic disc drusen"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem describes the finding and then asks for its name.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 130-135 divide neural rim thinning into two shapes: \"**Focal → polar notch → superior, inferior**\" and \"Circumferential → generalized thinning of the neural rim\". A **focal** loss of rim is a **notch**, and the clock hours fix where: 6 and 7 o'clock is the **inferior and infero-temporal** sector of the disc.\n\n**The field defect confirms the location, because the nerve fibre layer inverts the image.** Fibres entering the **inferior** rim carry the **superior** field, so a notch at 6-7 o'clock produces a **superior** arcuate scotoma — which is exactly what the perimetry shows. The inversion itself is *not taken from the course material*; `L11` line 151 lists \"Arcuate scotomata (superior, inferior)\" without saying which rim produces which.\n\n**The module photographs this notch twice without naming it.** `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 12, photograph 1: the rim is \"thinned, and **NOTCHED at the inferior pole**, where the rim tissue is lost and the cup reaches the margin\"; slide 13, photograph 2: \"a narrow rim all round, **thinnest inferiorly**\". The bank's own `opmcq-c11-19` prints \"Notching of the cup edge **esp. inferotemporally**\" as a characteristic of the glaucomatous disc — the same fact, from the other side.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Temporal pallor** | A **colour** change in the rim that remains, not a **loss** of rim. `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 14 makes pallor the language of optic **atrophy**, whose four types it captions Primary, Secondary, Consecutive and Glaucomatous; temporal pallor classically marks a papillomacular bundle lesion — demyelination, toxic and nutritional neuropathies — and goes with a **central or centrocaecal** defect, not an arcuate one *(the pairing is not taken from the course material)* |\n| **B. Inferotemporal notching** | The key |\n| **C. Peripapillary atrophy** | A real glaucoma sign and a real photograph in this module — `L21` slide 12 records \"a pale, mottled crescent of atrophy\" below and temporal to the disc — but it is a change in the retina **beside** the disc, not in the rim, and it does not produce a corresponding arcuate scotoma. ⚠️ **It is the strongest distractor here precisely because it often accompanies the notch** |\n| **D. Optic disc drusen** | Calcified hyaline bodies in a **crowded, small** disc with **no cup at all**. This disc is cupped, which is the opposite finding. Drusen also occur in younger patients and typically give an enlarged blind spot |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Focal rim loss in glaucoma is a notch, commonest at the inferior and infero-temporal pole; because the nerve fibre layer inverts the field, an inferior notch gives a SUPERIOR arcuate scotoma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.74 (Ch.11 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"What is the most accurate definition of glaucoma?",
    options:["Increased IOP above normal with / without structural damage to optic disc","Structural damage to optic disc and functional damage in form of visual field changed with / without elevation of IOP","Structural damage to optic disc and functional damage in form of visual field changed with elevation of IOP","Decreased IOP below normal"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ *Options B and C both print \"functional damage in form of **visual field changed**\", ungrammatically and identically. Transcribed as printed, verified at 400 dpi. It does not touch the key: B and C differ in exactly four words, \"with / without\" against \"with\", and that is the entire question.*\n\n**The question is asking which definition puts the NERVE first and the PRESSURE second, and both decks do.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` line 61: \"Glaucoma is a specific type of **optic neuropathy** in which intraocular pressure (IOP) is a **significant risk factor**.\" A risk factor, not a criterion. The deck then spells out why the pressure cannot be in the definition, at lines 68-71 — there are **exceptions in both directions**:\n\n> • eyes with optic neuropathy and **normal IOP** (normal tension glaucoma)\n> • eyes with **normal optic nerves and elevated IOP** (ocular hypertension)\n\nThose two lines are the whole answer. If elevated IOP were required, normal tension glaucoma would not be glaucoma; if elevated IOP were sufficient, ocular hypertension would be.\n\n**`L35,36) Chronic visual loss` prints the formal version and it contains no pressure at all.** The European Glaucoma Society definition, quoted there: \"progressive **optic neuropathies**, that have in common characteristic morphological changes at the **optic nerve head and retinal fiber layer** in the absence of other ocular disease or congenital anomalies. Progressive retinal **ganglion cell death and visual field loss** are associated with these changes.\" The same deck adds the clinical consequence: \"glaucomatous optic nerve changes are evident **despite an apparently 'normal' pressure**\" in many cases, and therefore \"examination of the **optic nerve** is the most important way to detect glaucoma\", with IOP a **screening** tool.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Increased IOP above normal with / without structural damage** | Puts the **pressure** in the definition and makes the disc optional. That is **ocular hypertension**, which `L11` line 71 names explicitly as something glaucoma is *not* |\n| **B. Structural damage to optic disc and functional damage in form of visual field changed with / without elevation of IOP** | The key. Structure **and** function, pressure optional — which is the only formulation that admits normal tension glaucoma |\n| **C. …with elevation of IOP** | ⚠️ **The trap, and it is one word away from the key.** It is the deck's own opening \"triad\" (`L11` lines 52-58: elevated IOP, optic nerve affection, visual field defects) taken as a *requirement*. The deck prints the triad first and then corrects it — the triad is a teaching scaffold, the line-61 sentence is the definition |\n| **D. Decreased IOP below normal** | Hypotony, the opposite condition |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Glaucoma is defined by the optic neuropathy — structural disc damage plus functional field loss — with IOP a significant risk factor rather than a criterion; that is what admits normal-tension glaucoma and excludes ocular hypertension.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.74 (Ch.11 Q3; options B and C both print "visual field changed")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following is considered a visual field defect that is characteristic of glaucoma?",
    options:["Floaters","Arcuate scotomata","Bitemporal hemianopia","Xanthopsia"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 148-155 print the glaucomatous field in six items, in the order the disease produces them:**\n\n> • Paracentral scotomata in **Bjerrum's area** (10 - 20 degrees of central field)\n> • **Arcuate scotomata** (superior, inferior)\n> • **Nasal step**\n> • **Ring scotoma** (double arcuate scotoma)\n> • Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field)\n> • Temporal island of vision\n\nArcuate scotomata are the second entry, and they are the only item on that list that appears in this menu. The same deck prints them again on its summary diagram (lines 38-41: \"Bjerrum scotoma · Arcuate scotoma · Nasal step · Ring scotoma\").\n\n**Why the arc:** the defect follows the **arcuate course of the nerve fibre bundles** as they sweep around the macula to enter the disc above and below, which is why the scotoma arches rather than running straight *(the fibre anatomy is not taken from the course material — `L11` names the defect but never the bundle)*.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Floaters** | Not a field defect at all — an **entoptic** symptom, opacities moving in the vitreous. `opmcq-c11-13` is the bank's own question separating the two, and it keys **scotomata** over floaters for the same reason |\n| **B. Arcuate scotomata** | The key |\n| **C. Bitemporal hemianopia** | A **chiasmal** defect, from a lesion at the crossing fibres — pituitary adenoma, craniopharyngioma. It respects the vertical meridian; a glaucomatous defect respects the **horizontal** one, which is what a nasal step is. This belongs to `op-neuro` and its deck `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` |\n| **D. Xanthopsia** | Yellow vision — a **colour** disturbance, classically from digoxin. Not a field defect and not glaucomatous *(the digoxin association is not taken from the course material; \"xanthopsia\" returns zero hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters)* |\n\n*The full six-item field sequence, and why it is silent until late, is written in `op-glauc`'s theory section on functional change; the printout version of this same defect is `opmcq-c11-25`.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The arcuate scotoma is the characteristic glaucomatous field defect, second in the deck's six-item sequence; bitemporal hemianopia is chiasmal, floaters are entoptic and xanthopsia is a colour disturbance.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.74 (Ch.11 Q4)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"What is the percentage of aqueous humor that is drained by the uveo-scleral pathway?",
    options:["10%","20%","50%","90%"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The number exists in exactly one place in this module, and it is not in either glaucoma deck.** `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` **slide 25** is a flow chart headed *Aqueous Outflow*, splitting from the posterior chamber into two pathways:\n\n> **LEFT PATHWAY:** Anterior chamber → Trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's Canal → Episcleral Veins → **Trabecular pathway (conventional) outflow = 90 %**\n> **RIGHT PATHWAY:** Ciliary body → Suprachoroidal space → Venous circulation → **Uveoscleral (unconventional) outflow = 10 %**\n\nThe two end boxes are highlighted on the slide. ⚠️ **This is a figure, not prose** — a grep of the glaucoma decks for a percentage returns nothing, and `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 105-106 give the drainage route as \"trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's canal → collector channels → systemic circulation\" with the uveoscleral route not mentioned at all. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 98-100 print the same route, also without it.\n\n**The book confirms the pathway exists but prints no share.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.9 labels a **\"Uveoscleral drainage route\"** inside a figure, and the cache flags that the route is \"taught **nowhere in this chapter's prose** — only in this figure\". `ophthalmology.pdf` p.131 adds the anatomy from the iris side: \"The iris along with the anterior ciliary body provide a **secondary pathway** for aqueous humour to drain from the eye (the **uveo-scleral pathway**)\" — secondary, and unquantified.\n\n**Why 10 % is worth holding:** the unconventional route is **pressure-independent**, which is what prostaglandin analogues exploit — they lower IOP by increasing uveoscleral outflow *(the mechanism is not taken from the course material; `L11` line 212 places prostaglandin analogues under \"increasing aqueous humour drainage\" without naming the route)*.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. 10 %** | The key — the uveoscleral (unconventional) share |\n| **B. 20 %** | Not printed anywhere in the module |\n| **C. 50 %** | Would make the two routes equal; the slide makes them nine to one |\n| **D. 90 %** | The **trabecular** share, and the key to `opmcq-c11-6`, which prints this identical menu on the facing question |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Uveoscleral (unconventional) outflow carries 10 % of the aqueous and the trabecular (conventional) route carries 90 %, a split printed only on the anatomy deck's outflow flow chart.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.75 (Ch.11 Q5; the bank hyphenates "uveo-scleral")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"What is the percentage of aqueous humor that is drained by the trabecular pathway?",
    options:["10%","20%","50%","90%"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The other half of the split, from the same flow chart.** `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` **slide 25** prints \"Anterior chamber → Trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's Canal → Episcleral Veins → **Trabecular pathway (conventional) outflow = 90 %**\". The full quotation, the book's corroboration and the reason this number lives on the anatomy deck rather than on either glaucoma deck are set out in `opmcq-c11-5`, which prints this identical menu and keys the 10 %.\n\n⚠️ **This is the bank pairing two questions on one menu, not printing one question twice.** The four options are the same four strings in the same order; the discriminating word is **\"trabecular\"** against **\"uveo-scleral\"**, and it moves the key from A to D.\n\n**Why the conventional route matters more than its share suggests.** It is the route every glaucoma deck names and the route glaucoma obstructs: `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 112-113 give the pathology of primary open angle glaucoma as \"**Increased resistance to aqueous humour drainage → trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's canal → collector channels → systemic circulation**\", and its four secondary open-angle causes (lines 159-173) each clog the same meshwork — pseudoexfoliative material, dispersed iris pigment, steroid-induced impermeability, inflammatory cells and plasmoid aqueous. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 47-55 define the whole open-versus-closed axis by whether the meshwork is \"**visibly exposed** to the aqueous humour\" on gonioscopy. Nine-tenths of the outflow passes through the structure the disease attacks.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. 10 %** | The **uveoscleral** share — the key to `opmcq-c11-5` |\n| **B. 20 %** | Not printed anywhere in the module |\n| **C. 50 %** | Not printed anywhere in the module |\n| **D. 90 %** | The key |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The trabecular (conventional) pathway drains 90 % of the aqueous — the route obstructed in primary and secondary open-angle glaucoma and the one gonioscopy is looking for.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.75 (Ch.11 Q6; identical option menu to Ch.11 Q5, opposite key)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 48-year-old female patient complains of diminished vision and severe pain in her left eye. Examination revealed marked ciliary injection, shallow anterior chamber, with iris bombe'. IOP was found to be 65 mmHg. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Acute angle closure glaucoma","Subacute angle closure glaucoma","Chronic angle closure glaucoma","Primary open angle glaucoma","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ *\"iris bombe'\" is printed as transcribed — a right single quotation mark stands where the acute accent of \"bombé\" belongs, verified at 400 dpi. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` prints it unaccented (\"iris bombe\", line 175) and `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` prints \"IRIS BOMBÉ\".*\n\n**Four of the stem's five findings are on one printed list of signs.** `L12` lines 202-217, *Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma — Signs*:\n\n> • Diminution of vision\n> • **IOP → ++++ (stony hard)**\n> • Corneal oedema (fundus examination, gonioscopy → usually not possible)\n> • **Ciliary injection**\n> • Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil\n> • **Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe (forward bowing)**\n> • Eyelid oedema\n\nAnd the symptom the stem opens with is the first of the printed symptoms: `L12` line 192, \"**Pain → ++++**\", with diminution of vision \"rapid (over several hours)\". An IOP of **65 mmHg** against a normal ceiling of 21 is the \"stony hard\" of that list.\n\n**The mechanism the stem is describing, in the deck's own chain** (`L12` lines 165-187): mid-dilated pupil → **relative pupil block** → aqueous accumulates behind the iris → **iris bombe** → narrowing of the angle → increased outflow resistance → **rapid severe rise of IOP**. The deck labels this chain \"**Ocular emergency**\". Iris bombé is therefore not an incidental sign here — it is the step in the mechanism that names the disease.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Acute angle closure glaucoma** | The key. `L12` line 134 places it second on the disease continuum: intermittent angle closure → **acute angle closure (crisis)** → chronic narrow angle glaucoma → absolute glaucoma |\n| **B. Subacute angle closure glaucoma** | ⚠️ **The trap, and the deck defines it by what this patient does NOT have.** `L12` lines 136-149, *Intermittent (subacute) Angle Closure Attacks*: \"Short episodes of angle closure\", \"**Mild** symptoms\", \"**Minimal or no blurring** of vision\", coloured haloes, brow ache, and \"attacks **resolve spontaneously**\" with bright illumination or sleep. Severe pain, vision loss and 65 mmHg are not mild and are not resolving |\n| **C. Chronic angle closure glaucoma** | `L12` lines 252-265 make it the *end* of repeated attacks, with a clinical picture \"**similar to primary open angle glaucoma** (elevation of IOP, progressive optic nerve cupping and visual field changes)\" — i.e. a quiet eye, not a crisis |\n| **D. Primary open angle glaucoma** | Fails on the chamber and on the eye. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 181-186 make POAG \"most commonly **asymptomatic**\", and by definition (`L12` lines 49-51) its angle is open and its chamber is not shallow. `L37) Red Eye` states the contrast as a rule: \"the more common chronic open-angle glaucoma causes **no redness** of the eye\" |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails, A being correct |\n\n*The book's red-eye grid agrees on every count: `ophthalmology.pdf` Table 1-2 gives acute glaucoma pain +++, vision markedly decreased, injection **ciliary**, cornea cloudy, IOP **markedly increased**, anterior chamber **shallow** — the only column in the table with a shallow chamber.*\n\n**This patient is the antecedent of the next four questions.** `opmcq-c11-8`, `-9`, `-10` and `-11` all print \"In the previous case\" and all refer back here — a four-deep chain, the longest in the chapter, walked on the page rather than assumed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Severe pain, ciliary injection, a shallow chamber with iris bombé and an IOP of 65 is the acute angle-closure crisis; the subacute form is mild and self-resolving and the chronic form looks like open-angle glaucoma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.75 (Ch.11 Q7; the stem prints "iris bombe\'" with a right single quote for the accent)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 48-year-old woman had severe pain and diminished vision in the left eye, with marked ciliary injection, a shallow anterior chamber, iris bombé and an IOP of 65 mmHg. Which of the following is an expected complication if her condition is left untreated?",
    options:["Permanent vision loss","Permanent peripheral anterior synechiae","A & B together","Neither A nor B"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following is an expected complication if the patient's condition is left untreated?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c11-7`, printed immediately above on book p.75. The restatement above repeats that patient's **findings** and stops there — it does not name acute angle closure glaucoma, because naming it would answer `opmcq-c11-7`.\n\n**Both consequences are printed, in different places, and the key is the pair.**\n\n**Permanent vision loss** is where the deck's own continuum ends. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 266-277 print four stages — intermittent angle closure → acute angle closure (crisis) → chronic narrow angle glaucoma → **absolute glaucoma** — and under absolute glaucoma the single caption **\"Blind painful eye\"**. That is the untreated endpoint, printed in two words.\n\n**Permanent peripheral anterior synechiae** are how the angle stops re-opening. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy` **slide 24** is a gonioscopic photograph whose arrow label reads \"**Peripheral anterior synechiae** ← arrow to the tissue bridging the angle\", set beside Schwalbe's line, the trabecular meshwork, the scleral spur and the ciliary band; the preceding slide captions the sequence \"Open Angle ⇢ Narrow Angle ⇢ **Closed Angle**\". `L12` lines 258-262 then supply the mechanism in words: \"**Repeated episodes of intermittent / acute angle closure attacks** → **Chronic closure of the angle**\". ⚠️ The deck does not use the phrase *peripheral anterior synechiae* for that chronic closure; the identification of the adhesions as PAS comes from the anatomy deck's figure and from general knowledge *(not taken from the course material)* — apposed iris and trabeculum become adherent, and adherent angle is angle that no longer drains.\n\n**Why both, and why \"permanent\":** the pressure damages the nerve while it is high, and the adhesions make the closure structural rather than functional, so relieving the pupil block afterwards no longer restores outflow. That is exactly why the bank asks for the **definitive** treatment two questions later (`opmcq-c11-11`) and why an iridotomy is done urgently.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Permanent vision loss** | True but incomplete — absolute glaucoma, `L12` line 277 |\n| **B. Permanent peripheral anterior synechiae** | True but incomplete — chronic closure of the angle, `L12` lines 258-262 |\n| **C. A & B together** | The key |\n| **D. Neither A nor B** | Contradicted by the deck's own continuum, whose whole point is that an untreated attack does not simply stop |\n\n*Grade Gain asks the mirror of this from the treatment end: `opqb-t11-477` keys \"falling field\" as the reason a patient on antiglaucoma treatment is sent for surgery, with PAS printed as a distractor.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"An untreated angle-closure attack runs to absolute glaucoma — a blind painful eye — and to permanent peripheral anterior synechiae that close the angle structurally; both, which is why the key is the pair.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.75 (Ch.11 Q8; printed "In the previous case, which of the following is an expected complication if the patient\'s condition is left untreated?" and refers back to Ch.11 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 48-year-old woman presents with severe pain and diminished vision in the left eye, marked ciliary injection, a shallow anterior chamber, iris bombé and an IOP of 65 mmHg. Which of the following is the initial medication used in order to rapidly control the condition?",
    options:["Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Topical prostaglandin analogues","Topical cholinergics","All of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following is the initial medication used in order to rapidly control the condition?\", and the reference reaches TWO questions back.** `opmcq-c11-8` sits between this question and its antecedent, but it is itself a back-reference and introduces no patient, so \"the previous case\" is still the woman of `opmcq-c11-7`. The chain was walked on the page.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **The bank printed this question twice, eleven apart, and this entry holds both printings** — same four options, same order, same key. The second, on p.78, asked it bare. The p.78 printing asked it without the case wrapper, which is why the two are held as one question rather than two. Flagged rather than merged.\n\n**Acetazolamide heads the deck's medical protocol, and acetazolamide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 218-232, *Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma — Treatment - Medical*:\n\n> • Hospitalisation\n> • **IOP lowering Rx → Systemic acetazolamide** · Topical beta-blockers · Topical alpha agonists · **Topical CAIs** · Topical miotics (pilocarpine)\n> • Hyperosmotic agents → IV mannitol · Oral hypertonic glycerol\n> • Topical steroids\n\nThe carbonic anhydrase inhibitor appears **twice** on that list — systemically first and topically fourth — and it is the only drug class on the list that does. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` line 210 gives the mechanism: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors act by \"**decreasing aqueous humour production**\", which is the fastest lever available when the drainage route is mechanically shut.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors** | The key. Systemic acetazolamide is the first named agent in the deck's own protocol |\n| **B. Topical prostaglandin analogues** | ⚠️ **Not on the acute list at all.** `L11` line 212 places prostaglandin analogues under \"**increasing aqueous humour drainage**\" — the one thing that cannot work when the angle is occluded by iris. They are also slow, taking hours to weeks, and are the maintenance drug of open-angle disease *(the onset is not taken from the course material)* |\n| **C. Topical cholinergics** | ⚠️ **On the deck's list, but last, and not as the initial agent.** `L12` line 226 prints \"Topical miotics (pilocarpine)\" fifth of five IOP-lowering drugs. Pilocarpine constricts the pupil to pull the peripheral iris out of the angle, but at a pressure of 65 the sphincter is ischaemic and often will not respond until the pressure has already been brought down by other means *(the ischaemic-sphincter reason is not taken from the course material)* |\n| **D. All of the above** | Fails on B, which is absent from the acute protocol |\n\n*Grade Gain keys the same drug class in a vignette — `opqb-t11-491`, first line of treatment in a 50-year-old with headache, red eye, mid-dilated irreactive pupil and corneal haze — but against a menu of medical versus surgical options, not of drug classes.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Systemic acetazolamide — a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor — heads the medical protocol for the angle-closure crisis; prostaglandin analogues work on drainage and have no place while the angle is shut.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.75-76 (Ch.11 Q9; printed "In the previous case…" and refers back TWO questions, to Ch.11 Q7. SEAM: options A-C close p.75, option D and the key open p.76)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 48-year-old woman with severe pain, marked ciliary injection, a shallow anterior chamber, iris bombé and an IOP of 65 mmHg in the left eye is also known to be diabetic. Which of the following medications would be contraindicated?",
    options:["Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Mannitol","Glycerine","Beta blockers"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, if the patient addressed is known to be diabetic, which of the following medications would be contraindicated?\"** The reference reaches THREE questions back, to `opmcq-c11-7`; `opmcq-c11-8` and `opmcq-c11-9` introduce no patient of their own.\n\n**All four options are on the deck's acute protocol, so the question is decided entirely by the diabetes.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 218-232 list systemic acetazolamide, topical beta-blockers, topical alpha agonists, topical CAIs, topical miotics, and then the hyperosmotic agents: \"**IV mannitol · Oral hypertonic glycerol**\".\n\n**Glycerol is the one that is metabolised.** Oral hyperosmotic glycerol is broken down as a carbohydrate and raises blood glucose, so in a diabetic it risks hyperglycaemia and ketoacidosis; **mannitol is not metabolised** and is excreted unchanged by the kidney, which is why it is the hyperosmotic agent given instead *(the metabolic difference is **not taken from the course material** — `L12` prints both agents side by side with no caution attached to either)*. The bank asks the positive half of the same fact seven questions later at `opmcq-c11-17`, where **Mannitol** is keyed as the best line in a diabetic with acute angle closure — the two questions are a matched pair and each confirms the other's reading.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors** | No diabetic contraindication. Acetazolamide's cautions are sulfonamide allergy, renal impairment, and the metabolic acidosis and hypokalaemia it produces *(not taken from the course material)* |\n| **B. Mannitol** | The alternative, not the contraindication. Its real cautions are cardiac failure and renal impairment, from the volume load *(not taken from the course material)* |\n| **C. Glycerine** | The key |\n| **D. Beta blockers** | ⚠️ **Not a wrong answer for a wrong reason — it is a defensible answer that the question does not key, and it is worth being explicit about.** Systemically absorbed topical beta blockers can **mask the adrenergic warning symptoms of hypoglycaemia** in a diabetic, and are contraindicated outright in asthma and heart block *(not taken from the course material)*. The bank's key rests on the sharper, purely metabolic objection to glycerol, and the printed key is C |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Oral hyperosmotic glycerol is metabolised as a carbohydrate and is avoided in diabetics; mannitol is not metabolised and is the hyperosmotic agent used instead.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.76 (Ch.11 Q10; printed "In the previous case, if the patient addressed is known to be diabetic…" and refers back THREE questions, to Ch.11 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 48-year-old woman presented with severe pain, marked ciliary injection, a shallow anterior chamber, iris bombé and an IOP of 65 mmHg in the left eye. Which of the following is the definitive treatment?",
    options:["Lensectomy","Trabeculectomy","Laser iridotomy","Valve surgery","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following is the definitive treatment?\"** The reference reaches FOUR questions back, to `opmcq-c11-7`, and closes that chain. None of `opmcq-c11-8`, `-9` or `-10` introduces a patient.\n\n**The deck prints the surgical treatment as two items and marks which laser is standard.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 233-243, *Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma — Treatment - Surgical*:\n\n> • **Iridotomy** — **Laser (standard)** · Surgical\n> • Filtering surgery — Trabeculectomy\n\n**Why the iridotomy is definitive and the drugs are not.** The deck's own mechanism (lines 165-187) runs mid-dilated pupil → **relative pupil block** → aqueous trapped behind the iris → **iris bombe** → angle narrows → IOP rises. Medical treatment lowers the pressure; it does not remove the block. A hole through the peripheral iris lets aqueous pass from posterior to anterior chamber directly, the iris falls back, and the angle re-opens — the cause is gone rather than compensated. The same deck offers iridotomy as the treatment of the **intermittent** stage too (line 154), which is the point: it treats the mechanism, whatever stage the mechanism has reached.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Lensectomy** | The definitive treatment of a **lens-induced** angle closure, not a primary one. `L18) Cataract II` gives \"**urgent phaco**\" for **phacomorphic** glaucoma, where a swollen immature lens is itself the cause of the closure. This patient's block is pupillary, not lenticular. (`opmcq-c11-21` is the chapter's phacomorphic question) |\n| **B. Trabeculectomy** | The deck's **filtering** surgery, and `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 215-217 define what it does: \"creation of a fistula between the anterior chamber and the subconjunctival space\". It bypasses the angle instead of re-opening it, and is reserved for the eye whose angle is already permanently closed by synechiae or whose pressure will not settle. It is `L11`'s surgery for **open** angle glaucoma |\n| **C. Laser iridotomy** | The key — the deck marks laser as the **standard** route |\n| **D. Valve surgery** | \"Synthetic valve implantation\" appears once in the module, at `L12` line 499, as a treatment for **congenital** glaucoma with an opaque cornea. A drainage implant is a late-stage device for refractory glaucoma, not a first definitive step |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails, C being correct |\n\n*Grade Gain keys the same operation for the **fellow** eye — `opqb-t11-449`, \"the treatment of choice for the other eye in angle closure glaucoma\", keyed YAG laser iridotomy — which is the prophylactic use of the same procedure; `L12` line 121 lists \"**affected fellow eye**\" among the risk factors, which is why the other eye is treated at all.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Laser iridotomy is the definitive treatment of the angle-closure crisis because it abolishes the pupil block that causes it; trabeculectomy bypasses the angle and lensectomy treats a lens-induced closure.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.76 (Ch.11 Q11; printed "In the previous case, which of the following is the definitive treatment?" and refers back FOUR questions, to Ch.11 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 68-year-old male patient complains of seeing black spots in his visual field. History was taken and it showed that the patient also suffered migraine and nocturnal hypotension. IOP was measured and found to be 18 mmHg. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Primary open angle glaucoma","Normal tension glaucoma","Secondary open angle glaucoma","Primary angle closure glaucoma","Secondary angle closure glaucoma"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The pressure is normal and the field is not — which is the definition of the exception.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 68-70 print it as one of the two things the glaucoma triad has to make room for:\n\n> • Exceptions →\n>     • eyes with **optic neuropathy and normal IOP (normal tension glaucoma)**\n>     • eyes with normal optic nerves and elevated IOP (ocular hypertension)\n\nAn IOP of **18 mmHg** sits inside the normal **10-21 mmHg** (`L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` line 225; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.31). The black spots are field loss — see `opmcq-c11-13`, the bank's own next question, which names them scotomata. Neuropathy plus a normal number is normal tension glaucoma by the deck's own wording.\n\n**Why the deck's structure makes this answerable even though it never lists a risk factor.** `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` supplies the principle: \"glaucomatous optic nerve changes are evident **despite an apparently 'normal' pressure**\" in many cases, and therefore \"examination of the **optic nerve** is the most important way to detect glaucoma\", with IOP only \"a valuable means of **screening**\". A normal pressure never excludes the disease.\n\n⚠️ **The migraine and the nocturnal hypotension are supplied by the bank and are not taught in the glaucoma decks.** Measured: `nocturnal` returns hits only in `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` and `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.14, both about **non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy** — \"many occur during periods of nocturnal hypotension\" — and `migraine` returns hits only in `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.1 and ch.14 as a cause of transient visual loss. `L11` names normal tension glaucoma once and gives it no risk factors at all. The association is real and is worth holding — **vasospasm and low nocturnal perfusion pressure are the classic normal-tension risk factors, alongside female sex, low blood pressure and Raynaud phenomenon** — but it is **not taken from the course material**. Note the mechanism the module *does* teach for nocturnal hypotension is the ischaemic optic neuropathy of `L31,32`; the same nocturnal dip is being invoked here for a chronic nerve.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Primary open angle glaucoma** | ⚠️ **The trap, and it fails only on the number.** Everything else fits — painless, insidious, field loss before the patient notices. `L11` lines 114-124 give its predisposing factors as older age, female sex, dark races, family history and **myopia**, none of which is in this stem, and the disease is defined against an **elevated** IOP |\n| **B. Normal tension glaucoma** | The key |\n| **C. Secondary open angle glaucoma** | Requires a visible cause for the outflow resistance. `L11` lines 159-173 lists four — pseudoexfoliation, pigment dispersion, steroid-induced, uveitic — and the stem offers none |\n| **D. Primary angle closure glaucoma** | Would be painful, red and stony hard, with a shallow chamber. This eye is quiet at 18 mmHg |\n| **E. Secondary angle closure glaucoma** | Requires a cause for a narrow angle — `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 289-304 give neovascular glaucoma, iridocorneal endothelial syndrome, intraocular tumours and retinal vein occlusion. None is offered |\n\n*This patient is the antecedent of `opmcq-c11-13` and `opmcq-c11-14`. The bank prints this same five-option menu again at `opmcq-c11-16` and keys A there — a paired menu, not a reprint.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Glaucomatous field loss with an IOP inside the normal 10-21 mmHg is normal tension glaucoma, one of the two exceptions the deck prints against its own triad; a normal pressure never excludes the disease.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.76 (Ch.11 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 68-year-old man with normal tension glaucoma complains of seeing black spots in his visual field. Which of the following is the ocular symptom / sign that causes the patient to see black spots in his visual field?",
    options:["Scotomata","Floaters","Flashes","Glare","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following is the ocular symptom / sign that causes the patient to see black spots in his visual field?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c11-12`, printed immediately above on book p.76. ⚠️ The restatement above **does** name normal tension glaucoma, and that is deliberate: `opmcq-c11-12` has already been asked and answered on the page above, and without the diagnosis this question has two defensible answers rather than one — see the note on option B.\n\n**The book defines all four candidates in one list, and the definitions are the whole question.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.4, the symptom vocabulary:\n\n> 5. **Glare:** difficulty of seeing in the presence of bright light.\n> 8. **Photopsia:** seeing **flashes of light** in the absence of a light source.\n> 9. **Floaters:** seeing **floating black shadows** in bright illumination\n> 11. **Central Scotoma:** Seeing **dark spots within the visual field** of one or both eyes\n\n\"Black spots **in his visual field**\" is item 11 almost word for word.\n\n**And the diagnosis settles it.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 148-155 give the glaucomatous field as paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum's area, arcuate scotomata, nasal step, ring scotoma, constriction to a tubular field and a temporal island — six named scotomata and no other kind of symptom. Glaucoma destroys retinal ganglion cells, so the corresponding patch of field goes blind; the patient perceives an absence, fixed in position.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Scotomata** | The key |\n| **B. Floaters** | ⚠️ **The one option that shares the words and not the mechanism, and it is the reason the diagnosis had to stay in the restated stem.** The book's own definition — \"floating black shadows\" — is a black spot too. The discriminators are that floaters **move with the eye and drift**, appear **in bright illumination**, and are opacities *in the media* casting a shadow on a healthy retina; a scotoma is **fixed in the field**, does not drift, and is a dead patch of retina or nerve. Floaters belong to the vitreous — `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.1 lists them under age-related vitreous change — and nothing in `L11` or `L12` gives glaucoma a vitreous symptom |\n| **C. Flashes** | Photopsia, \"in the **absence** of a light source\" — a positive phenomenon from mechanical or ischaemic stimulation of the retina, classically vitreoretinal traction. The opposite of a black spot |\n| **D. Glare** | Difficulty seeing **in bright light**, a scattering symptom. `L18) Cataract II` makes it a cataract sign — \"glare prominent when opacities are\" central — not a glaucoma one |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails, A being correct |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A scotoma is a dark spot fixed within the visual field, from dead retina or nerve; floaters are drifting media opacities, photopsia is a flash without a light source and glare is difficulty seeing in bright light.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.76 (Ch.11 Q13; printed "In the previous case, which of the following is the ocular symptom / sign that causes the patient to see black spots in his visual field?" and refers back to Ch.11 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 68-year-old man with normal tension glaucoma has scotomata in his visual field. Which of the following is the end stage of his disease process?",
    options:["Visual field changes","Blurred vision","Permanent vision loss","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following is the end stage of the patient's disease process?\", and the reference reaches TWO questions back**, to `opmcq-c11-12`; `opmcq-c11-13` introduces no patient of its own. The restated stem carries forward both facts already established on the page above.\n\n**The question is asking where the deck's sequence ends, and the deck ends it in one line.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 146-157 order the functional damage: field changes first — paracentral scotomata, arcuate scotomata, nasal step, ring scotoma, constriction to a **tubular field**, a **temporal island of vision** — and then, and only then, \"Visual acuity changes → **Diminution of central vision in end stage disease**\". The phrase *end stage* is the deck's own.\n\n**Two more sources close the loop on \"permanent\".** `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` prints \"**Damage is irreversible**\" as one of four headline facts about glaucoma, and calls it \"the **leading cause of irreversible blindness**\". `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` line 277 gives the angle-closure version of the same endpoint — **absolute glaucoma**, captioned \"**Blind painful eye**\". Ganglion cells that die do not regenerate, which is why the whole management of glaucoma is preventive: `L11` lines 204-205 set the aim as \"**preserve** visual function to beyond the patient life expectancy\".\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Visual field changes** | The **beginning** of the sequence, not its end — and this patient already has them, which is what `opmcq-c11-13` established |\n| **B. Blurred vision** | The middle. `L11` line 157 puts diminution of central vision at the end stage, so blurring is the last warning rather than the outcome |\n| **C. Permanent vision loss** | The key |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails, C being correct |\n\n*⚠️ The bank uses the same three-stage logic in the angle-closure chain: `opmcq-c11-8` keys permanent vision loss AND permanent peripheral anterior synechiae as the untreated outcome there. The two questions are the chronic and the acute versions of one point.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Glaucoma takes the field first and central acuity only in end-stage disease, and the damage is irreversible — permanent vision loss is the endpoint, which is why treatment is preventive.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.77 (Ch.11 Q14; printed "In the previous case, which of the following is the end stage of the patient\'s disease process?" and refers back TWO questions, to Ch.11 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following fibres are the most vulnerable to glaucomatous damage?",
    options:["Nasal fibres","Arcuate fibres","Papillomacular bundle","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The nerve fibre bundles are named nowhere in this module.** Measured: **`papillomacular` returns ZERO hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters** (also tried \"papillo-macular\" and \"maculopapillar\"), and so do **`arcuate fibres`/`arcuate fibers`** and **`vulnerab`**. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` teaches the arcuate **scotoma** and never the bundle behind it. The bundle anatomy below is **not taken from the course material**.\n\n**But the module's own field sequence points at the same answer, and that is the stronger argument.** `L11` lines 148-157 order the damage:\n\n> • Paracentral scotomata in **Bjerrum's area** (10 - 20 degrees of central field)\n> • **Arcuate scotomata** (superior, inferior)\n> • Nasal step · Ring scotoma · Constriction of visual field · Temporal island of vision\n> • Visual acuity changes → Diminution of **central vision in END STAGE disease**\n\nThe first defects are arcuate, and the last thing to go is central acuity. A field defect is a map of which fibres have died, so a disease that produces arcuate scotomata first and spares central vision until the end is a disease that kills the **arcuate** bundles first and the **papillomacular** bundle last. The deck supplies the observation; the anatomy only names it.\n\n**The anatomy, tagged.** *Not taken from the course material:* retinal ganglion cell axons run in three groups — the **papillomacular bundle** straight from macula to the temporal disc, the **arcuate (temporal) fibres** arching above and below it to enter the superior and inferior poles, and the **nasal (radial) fibres** running straight in from the nasal retina. The arcuate fibres enter at the superior and inferior poles, where the lamina cribrosa's pores are largest and its connective-tissue support thinnest, and they are the ones lost first — which is why `L11` line 133 puts the **focal polar notch** \"superior, inferior\" and why `opmcq-c11-2` and `opmcq-c11-19` both make inferotemporal notching the disc sign.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Nasal fibres** | Damaged, but later; their loss is the **nasal step**, third on `L11`'s list |\n| **B. Arcuate fibres** | The key |\n| **C. Papillomacular bundle** | The **most resistant** — and the key to `opmcq-c11-24`, which prints this identical menu with \"resistant\" for \"vulnerable\" |\n| **D. All of the above** | Would deny the ordering the whole field sequence is built on |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails, B being correct |\n\n⚠️ *This question and `opmcq-c11-24` share all five options in the same order and key differently. One word — **vulnerable** against **resistant** — is the entire difference.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The arcuate nerve fibre bundles entering the superior and inferior disc poles are lost first in glaucoma — which is why arcuate scotomata come early — and the papillomacular bundle is lost last, which is why central acuity survives to end stage.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.77 (Ch.11 Q15; the bank prints the British "fibres")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common form of glaucoma?",
    options:["Primary open angle glaucoma","Normal tension glaucoma","Secondary open angle glaucoma","Primary angle closure glaucoma","Secondary angle closure glaucoma"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Two sources rank it, from opposite directions, and they agree.**\n\n`L35,36) Chronic visual loss`, under GLAUCOMA: \"The majority of Chronic visual loss are **primary open angle glaucoma** followed by secondary angle closure glaucoma.\" The same deck gives it the epidemiological weight — glaucoma is \"the **leading cause of irreversible blindness**\", with a 7.6-19.7 % prevalence of blindness among glaucoma patients, \"the **second** most important cause of blindness in the United States and the single most important cause of blindness in **African Americans**\".\n\n`L37) Red Eye` reaches the same conclusion by naming the other form uncommon: \"Acute angle-closure glaucoma is an **uncommon** form of glaucoma due to sudden and complete occlusion of the anterior chamber angle by iris tissue... The **more common chronic open-angle glaucoma** causes no redness of the eye.\"\n\n**Why it is the common one.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 110-124 give a disease with no obvious cause, a slow rise in trabecular outflow resistance, and a risk profile that most of an ageing population carries — age over the fifth decade, female sex, dark races, family history (\"**10-fold** increased risk in first degree relatives\") and myopia. Its presentation (lines 181-186) is \"most commonly **asymptomatic**\", so it accumulates undiagnosed; `L35,36` adds that it \"is often diagnosed in **advanced stages** especially in rural areas\".\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Primary open angle glaucoma** | The key |\n| **B. Normal tension glaucoma** | `L11` line 69 gives it one line, as an **exception** to the definition, and no prevalence at all |\n| **C. Secondary open angle glaucoma** | Requires one of four visible causes (`L11` lines 159-173) and is therefore a subset of eyes with those causes |\n| **D. Primary angle closure glaucoma** | Explicitly **uncommon** — `L37) Red Eye`, quoted above |\n| **E. Secondary angle closure glaucoma** | `L35,36` puts it **second**, not first, in its own ranking sentence |\n\n*⚠️ Where the module's own numbers pull against each other: `L35,36`'s ranking sentence is about the causes of **chronic visual loss**, not about glaucoma subtypes in the population, and `op-insid`'s theory records that as printed. As a ranking of glaucoma forms — which is what this question asks — it is consistent with `L37`, and both point to A. Identical five-option menu to `opmcq-c11-12`, different key.*\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-insid` \"Insidious Visual Loss\" — `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` is where the module's glaucoma epidemiology is taught, and this question's ranking comes from there. Filed `op-glauc` because what is tested is the classification of glaucoma, not the epidemiology of blindness.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Primary open angle glaucoma is the commonest form — the chronic visual loss deck ranks it first and the red eye chapter calls acute angle closure uncommon by contrast.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.77 (Ch.11 Q16; identical option menu to Ch.11 Q12, different key)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following is the best line of management of a diabetic patient presenting with acute angle closure glaucoma?",
    options:["Alpha blockers","Topical mydriatics","Mannitol","Glycerine"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**This is the positive mirror of `opmcq-c11-10`, seven questions earlier.** That question asked which drug a diabetic in an angle-closure crisis must NOT be given and keyed **Glycerine**; this one asks what to give instead, and keys the other hyperosmotic agent on the same printed list.\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 228-230, inside *Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma — Treatment - Medical*:\n\n> • **Hyperosmotic agents**\n>     • **IV mannitol**\n>     • Oral hypertonic glycerol\n\nThe deck prints both without distinguishing them. **Mannitol is not metabolised** and is excreted unchanged, whereas **oral glycerol is metabolised as a carbohydrate** and raises blood glucose — which is the whole of the difference in a diabetic *(the metabolic point is **not taken from the course material**)*. Hyperosmotic agents work by drawing water osmotically out of the vitreous, shrinking the vitreous volume and letting the iris-lens diaphragm fall back, so they lower a very high pressure faster than anything that acts on production or outflow *(mechanism also not taken from the course material)*.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Alpha blockers** | ⚠️ **Not a glaucoma drug in this material at all, and the option is worth flagging as printed.** Both decks give alpha **AGONISTS**: `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` line 210 lists them among the drugs that **decrease** aqueous production, line 212 among those that **increase** drainage, and `L12` line 224 prints \"Topical **alpha agonists**\" in the acute protocol. An alpha *blocker* appears nowhere. Transcribed as printed |\n| **B. Topical mydriatics** | ⚠️ **The dangerous option, and the reason the question exists.** Dilating the pupil is what precipitates the attack. `L12` lines 165-175 begin the mechanism at the \"**Mid-dilated position of the pupil**\" → relative pupil block → iris bombe, and lines 125-127 list \"Use of systemic medications (**anticholinergics**)\" and a \"**Dark environment**\" among the things that raise the risk. Grade Gain asks the same trap twice — `opqb-t11-450` keys topical **atropine** as contraindicated in primary angle closure glaucoma, and `opqb-t11-459` keys atropine as the drug that can cause closed angle glaucoma in predisposed eyes |\n| **C. Mannitol** | The key |\n| **D. Glycerine** | The contraindication — see `opmcq-c11-10` |\n\n*Grade Gain keys mannitol for a different reason at `opqb-t11-484`: it asks which IOP-lowering medication has the **strongest** effect, against prostaglandins, beta blockers and pilocarpine. Same drug, different question.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"IV mannitol is the hyperosmotic agent of choice in a diabetic with acute angle closure because it is not metabolised, unlike oral glycerol; a mydriatic is what precipitates the attack.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.77 (Ch.11 Q17; option A prints "Alpha blockers" where both decks print alpha agonists)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"All of the following are among the risk factors of primary angle closure glaucoma except?",
    options:["Female gender","Myopia","Southeast asians","Phenothiazines","Dark environments"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ *Option C prints \"Southeast asians\" with a lower-case demonym, transcribed as printed and verified at 400 dpi. `L12` prints \"Race (South-East Asians)\".*\n\n**`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 109-127 print the risk factors as two separate questions, and four of the five options are on them.**\n\n> **Who is at risk for angle closure glaucoma? (crowded anterior segments)**\n> **Females** · **Hyperopes** · **Race (South-East Asians)** · Advancing age · Affected fellow eye\n>\n> **What increases the risk of angle closure glaucoma? (mid-dilated pupil)**\n> Use of systemic medications (**anticholinergics**) · **Dark environment**\n\n**The odd one out is myopia, and it is not merely absent — it belongs to the other disease.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 123-124 give the refractive risk for **primary open angle** glaucoma: \"Refractive state → **More common in myopia**.\" The angle-closure risk is the opposite refractive error, because a **hyperopic eye is short**, so a normally sized lens sits in a crowded anterior segment. Grade Gain asks that half directly at `opqb-t11-485` and keys the **hypermetropic** eye. `L7,8) Refractive errors` says it from the hyperopia side: \"Predisposition to narrow angle glaucoma due to shallow anterior chamber (especially later in life as the lens becomes thicker).\"\n\n⚠️ **Phenothiazines are the one option the module never prints.** Measured: `phenothiazin` returns **ZERO hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters**. (The word appears once in the live corpus, at `opmcq-c9-24`, as a cause of **cataract**.) The bridge to `L12`'s printed \"anticholinergics\" is that **phenothiazine antipsychotics have substantial antimuscarinic activity, dilate the pupil and can precipitate an attack in an occludable angle** — *not taken from the course material*.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Female gender** | `L12` line 120, \"Females\", first in the crowded-anterior-segment row |\n| **B. Myopia** | **The key.** The refractive risk for the OTHER glaucoma — `L11` line 124 |\n| **C. Southeast asians** | `L12` line 119, \"Race (South-East Asians)\" |\n| **D. Phenothiazines** | Anticholinergic mydriasis — `L12` line 127 names the class, not the drug |\n| **E. Dark environments** | `L12` line 126, \"Dark environment\", and the reason attacks begin in the evening — a dim room mid-dilates the pupil, which is where `L12` line 166 starts the mechanism |\n\n*⚠️ Why MID-dilated and not fully dilated: the peripheral iris is at its most bunched into the angle while pupillary block is still substantial. That mechanism is **not taken from the course material**; the deck prints only the phrase \"mid-dilated pupil\" as the heading of its risk column.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Angle closure is the disease of the crowded, HYPEROPIC eye — female, South-East Asian, ageing, with a dark room or an anticholinergic to mid-dilate the pupil; myopia is the refractive risk factor for primary OPEN angle glaucoma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.77-78 (Ch.11 Q18; option C prints "Southeast asians". SEAM: stem and all five options close p.77, the key is orphaned at the top of p.78)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"All of the following are among the characteristics of glaucomatous optic disc except?",
    options:["Nasal shifting of blood vessels","Notching of the cup edge esp. inferotemporally","Optic cup becomes horizontally oval","Asymmetry of the size of the right and left optic disc cups > 0.2"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ *Option B prints the abbreviation \"esp.\" mid-option; option D prints \"> 0.2\". Both transcribed as printed.*\n\n**Three of the four are printed characteristics, one from each of the module's three sources.**\n\n**A — nasal shifting of the vessels.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 139-144, under *Additional features*: \"Splinter haemorrhage\" and \"**Total cupping with nasal shift of blood vessels**\". `L21) Optic Nerve` photographs it — slide 10, \"the trunk emerges **NASALLY**, at the edge of the cup\"; slide 12, \"the trunk is **displaced towards the nasal edge**\".\n\n**B — notching, especially inferotemporally.** `L11` lines 132-133: \"Neural rim thinning → structural changes · **Focal → polar notch → superior, inferior**\". `L21` slide 12 photographs a rim \"thinned, and **NOTCHED at the inferior pole**\" and slide 13 a rim \"**thinnest inferiorly**\". `opmcq-c11-2` is the same fact as a vignette.\n\n**D — an inter-eye difference greater than 0.2.** `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.36**: \"The ratio of the optic cup to the overall diameter of the optic disc (**C/D ratio**)... An average ratio range is **0.2-0.4**. **C/D ratios more than 0.5** or **dissimilarity of C/D ratios of more than 0.2 between both eyes** (e.g., 0.2 and 0.5) require referral to an ophthalmologist.\" Glaucoma is usually asymmetric, so the difference between the eyes is a sign in its own right.\n\n**C is the exception, and it is a direction error.** The glaucomatous cup enlarges **VERTICALLY**, not horizontally: the rim is lost first at the superior and inferior poles (`L11` line 133's \"polar notch → superior, inferior\"), so the excavation extends up and down and the cup becomes **vertically** oval. ⚠️ **The module never prints this in words.** Measured: `oval cup` returns zero hits across all 29 cached decks and all 12 cached book chapters, and `vertically oval` returns hits only for the **PUPIL** of acute angle closure (`L12` line 213; `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.1). The nearest printed statement is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.36 on the **disc**: \"It is slightly **oval in the vertical meridian**.\" The vertical enlargement of the **cup** is *not taken from the course material* — but the polar notching that produces it is printed twice, so the deck supplies the mechanism even where it does not supply the phrase.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Nasal shifting of blood vessels** | Printed — `L11` line 144 |\n| **B. Notching of the cup edge esp. inferotemporally** | Printed — `L11` line 133 |\n| **C. Optic cup becomes horizontally oval** | **The key.** Vertical, not horizontal |\n| **D. Asymmetry of the size of the right and left optic disc cups > 0.2** | Printed — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.36 |\n\n*⚠️ Held divergence, recorded not resolved: `L11` line 137 sets the suspicious cup-disc ratio at \"**> 0.3**\", `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` at \"**0.5 or more**\", and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.36 at a normal range of **0.2-0.4** with referral above 0.5. The module's theory already carries this divergence; no option here turns on it.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The glaucomatous disc shows polar (especially inferotemporal) notching, nasal shift of the vessels and inter-eye cup asymmetry greater than 0.2 — and its cup enlarges VERTICALLY, not horizontally.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.78 (Ch.11 Q19)' },

  /* opmcq-c11-20 WAS FOLDED, NOT WRITTEN -- 2026-08-25.
     A reprint of `opmcq-c11-9` (book p.75) on p.78: the same four options in the
     same order with the same key, and NO TEXTUAL DIFFERENCE ANYWHERE IN THE
     OPTION LIST -- the first fold on this module decided on the ASK alone.
     Q9 frames it as a vignette and asks for the drug that RAPIDLY CONTROLS the
     pressure; Q20 asks bare for the FIRST LINE. In acute angle closure those are
     the same drug, and Q9 names iris bombe, a shallow chamber and an IOP of 65,
     so its vignette hands the diagnosis over rather than testing it.
     Q9 held: the fuller printing, 2,930 explanation characters against 1,814.
     No `alsoIn`: one bank reprinting itself is not two banks printing it.
     No marker consequence: the chapter prints no box, so both carried one. */
  { id:'opmcq-c11-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following conditions is among the causes of secondary angle closure glaucoma?",
    options:["Traumatic glaucoma","Inflammatory glaucoma","Steroid-induced glaucoma","Phacomorphic glaucoma"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The question is testing one axis only: for each named secondary glaucoma, is the angle OPEN or CLOSED?** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 47-55 define the axis — open angle means the trabecular meshwork is \"visibly exposed to the aqueous humour\" on gonioscopy; closed angle means it is not. Three of these four block an angle that stays open; one closes it.\n\n**D — phacomorphic glaucoma closes the angle mechanically.** `L18) Cataract II` prints the chain: \"Phacomorphic: **swollen immature lens → angle closure**\", and its summary table gives \"Phacomorphic Glaucoma · Immature cataract · **Swollen lens → angle closure** · Red eye + pain + HM · Raised IOP, corneal oedema · Urgent phaco.\" `L17) Cataract I` lists it among the complications of cataract. A thickened lens pushes the iris forward, the angle shuts, and the mechanism is anatomical rather than obstructive.\n\n**A — traumatic glaucoma blocks an open angle.** `L25) Trauma I` slide 27 gives hyphema's complication as \"**Uncontrolled high IOP** which can cause optic nerve damage, blood staining of the cornea, CRAO\", and slide 26 explains the mechanism as \"impaired aqueous circulation\" — red cells obstructing the meshwork. (Measured: `traumatic glaucoma` and `angle recession` both return **zero** hits across all 29 decks and 12 book chapters; the mechanism above is the module's, the classification of it as open-angle is *not taken from the course material*.)\n\n**B — inflammatory glaucoma blocks an open angle, and the book says so explicitly.** `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.135**: \"Sometimes the IOP increases [**Secondary inflammatory glaucoma**] due to: • **Trabeculitis** as in HSV or HZV iritis • **Plasmoid aqueous and circulating large-sized WBCs obstruct the trabecular meshwork** resulting in impaired aqueous drainage.\" Both mechanisms are trabecular, i.e. open angle. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 171-173 file \"Uveitic\" under **Secondary open angle glaucoma** for exactly that reason: \"Deposition of inflammatory cells and plasmoid (viscid) aqueous humour → decreased permeability of the trabecular meshwork.\"\n\n**C — steroid-induced glaucoma blocks an open angle.** `L11` lines 168-170, also under *Secondary open angle glaucoma*: \"Prolonged intake of potent steroids in a predisposed individual → **decreased permeability of the trabecular meshwork** to aqueous humour.\"\n\n⚠️ **The chapter contradicts itself four questions later, and it is recorded rather than resolved.** `opmcq-c11-22` prints \"Posterior synechiae due to uveitis\" as a cause of secondary **angle closure** glaucoma and keys a list that includes it — while this question's key excludes \"Inflammatory glaucoma\" from that same category. Both readings are defensible on the material: uveitis raises the IOP through the meshwork (open angle, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135) **and**, when a ring of posterior synechiae seals the pupil, through seclusio pupillae and iris bombé (closed angle). Neither key is disputed.\n\n*⚠️ Filing note: the key is a **lens-induced** glaucoma, and the project files phacomorphic and phacolytic in `op-cat` \"Cataract — types, maturity and clinical picture\", whose theory says in as many words that `op-glauc` \"is written from `L11` and `L12`, which print no lens-induced glaucoma at all\". This question is filed `op-glauc` because what it tests is the **open-versus-closed classification of secondary glaucoma** — its three distractors are `L11`'s secondary open-angle list — not the cataract that supplies the answer. **(Secondary chapter: `op-cat` — the phacomorphic mechanism itself is taught there, from `L17` and `L18`.)***\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Traumatic, inflammatory and steroid-induced glaucomas all obstruct an OPEN angle at the trabecular meshwork; phacomorphic glaucoma is a swollen lens pushing the iris forward and closing the angle mechanically.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.78 (Ch.11 Q21)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"All of the following can cause secondary angle closure glaucoma except?",
    options:["Posterior synechiae due to uveitis","PDR","CRVO","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **PRINTED DEFECT, recorded and not corrected: the stem says \"except\" and the key is \"All of the above\".** An \"except\" stem asks which option does **not** belong, and \"All of the above\" is not a candidate for that; read literally the question has no answer, and read as an \"except\" the only defensible option would be **E**. The internally consistent reading is that the stem kept an \"except\" that the question outgrew — every one of A, B and C **is** a genuine cause of secondary angle closure, and as a positive question the key is exactly right. `answer` is the bank's printed key, per the project rule of 2026-08-11; the disagreement is recorded here and never disputed. *(⚠️ M5: the bank also prints a blank line between option D and option E, the only option list in the chapter not set solid. Verified at 400 dpi. Cosmetic — no letter is skipped or duplicated.)*\n\n**Taking the three causes in turn.**\n\n**C — CRVO is printed in the deck's own list, with its mechanism.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 302-304, under *Secondary Narrow angle glaucoma*: \"**Retinal Vein Occlusion** — Congestion of the retinal veins causes **forward rotation of the iris lens diaphragm**.\" That is the deck's \"**posterior pushing**\" mechanism (line 287).\n\n**B — PDR reaches the angle through neovascular glaucoma, which is the deck's other mechanism.** `L12` lines 290-292: \"**Neovascular Glaucoma** — Neovascular membrane grows over the trabecular meshwork and **contracts pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum**\" — the \"**anterior pulling**\" mechanism. Proliferative diabetic retinopathy is the archetypal driver of that membrane, through retinal ischaemia and rubeosis iridis. ⚠️ The link is **not spelled out in `L12`**: `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` records in its own negatives list that under rubeosis iridis \"**Diabetic retinopathy and central retinal vein occlusion are never named as causes; neovascular glaucoma is never named as the consequence**\". The ischaemia-to-rubeosis-to-neovascular-glaucoma chain is therefore *not taken from the course material*, although both ends of it are. Grade Gain prints the same chain twice — `opqb-t11-451` keys central retinal vein thrombosis as what neovascular glaucoma follows, and `opqb-t11-458` keys **branch retinal artery occlusion** as the least likely cause, against a menu that includes proliferative diabetic retinopathy.\n\n**A — posterior synechiae close the angle by sealing the pupil.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 lists \"Posterior synechiae → (leads to irregular shape of the iris)\" among the complications of anterior uveitis, and names **2ry glaucoma** immediately above it. ⚠️ The step between them — a ring of synechiae binding the whole pupil margin to the lens (**seclusio pupillae**) blocks aqueous from reaching the anterior chamber, the iris balloons forward as **iris bombé**, and the peripheral iris covers the angle — is **not printed in the module**: `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` records exactly that gap in its own negatives list, \"**SECLUSIO PUPILLAE, IRIS BOMBÉ and PUPILLARY BLOCK are not printed anywhere in the deck**, even though 'Secondary glaucoma' is listed as a complication\". *Not taken from the course material.* The end state is the one `L12` line 175 does print, iris bombe, reached by a different route from the primary attack.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Posterior synechiae due to uveitis** | A genuine cause — seclusio pupillae → iris bombé → angle closure |\n| **B. PDR** | A genuine cause — rubeosis → neovascular membrane → anterior pulling |\n| **C. CRVO** | A genuine cause — printed in `L12`'s own list, posterior pushing |\n| **D. All of the above** | **The printed key.** True as a positive statement, incoherent against the stem's \"except\" |\n| **E. None of the above** | The only option an \"except\" reading could take, and it is not keyed |\n\n⚠️ **See also `opmcq-c11-21`, four questions earlier**, whose key excludes \"Inflammatory glaucoma\" from the causes of secondary angle closure while this question's option A is uveitic. Both are defensible — uveitis raises the pressure through the meshwork *and* through the pupil — but the two questions cannot both be read as exhaustive. Recorded in both entries; neither key touched.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Secondary angle closure is either anterior pulling (a neovascular membrane, as in PDR) or posterior pushing (venous congestion in CRVO), plus pupillary seclusion from uveitic synechiae — all three, which is why the key is \"All of the above\" despite the stem's \"except\".",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.78 (Ch.11 Q22; the stem prints "except" and the key prints "Answer: D" = "All of the above", and a blank line is printed between options D and E)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"All of the following medications increase aqueous humor drainage except?",
    options:["Topical cholinergics","Topical alpha adrenergics","Topical beta blockers","Topical prostaglandin analgoues","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ *Option D prints \"prostaglandin **analgoues**\", transcribed as printed and verified at 400 dpi. The same word is spelled correctly three times elsewhere in this chapter — `opmcq-c11-9` option B, again in the p.78 reprint folded into that entry, and `opmcq-c11-26` option A — so the typo is local to this line.*\n\n**`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 209-212 sort every glaucoma drug into two boxes, and the sort is the answer:**\n\n> • Medical treatment →\n>     • **Decreasing aqueous humour production** → **beta-blockers**, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, alpha agonists\n>     • **Increasing aqueous humour drainage** → **prostaglandin analogues, miotics, alpha agonists**\n\nThree of the four named drugs in this menu are in the drainage box. **Beta-blockers appear only in the production box**, and they are the only class in the whole list that appears in one box and not the other.\n\n**The `L11` sort is worth reading closely, because one class is in BOTH boxes.** Alpha agonists cut production *and* improve outflow, which is why option B is not the exception even though a reader who remembers them as production-reducers might pick it. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` line 224 confirms they are in normal acute use as \"Topical alpha agonists\".\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Topical cholinergics** | The **miotics** of `L11` line 212 — `L12` line 226 names the drug, \"Topical miotics (**pilocarpine**)\". They contract the ciliary muscle and pull on the scleral spur, opening the meshwork *(the mechanism is not taken from the course material; `L11` prints only the box they sit in)* |\n| **B. Topical alpha adrenergics** | ⚠️ Printed in **both** of `L11`'s boxes — lines 210 and 212. Not the exception |\n| **C. Topical beta blockers** | **The key.** `L11` line 210 puts them in the production box and nowhere else. They act on the ciliary epithelium, cutting aqueous formation *(mechanism not taken from the course material)* |\n| **D. Topical prostaglandin analgoues** | First in `L11`'s drainage box, line 212 |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails, C being correct |\n\n⚠️ **CROSS-BANK: this question and `opqb-t11-469` ask the same thing and key the same drug.** Grade Gain prints \"All of these drugs increase aqueous drainage EXCEPT: Prostaglandins / Alpha adrenergic **blockers** / Cholinergic agonists / **Beta blockers**\" (`answer:3`). Three real differences separate the two printings, and they are the reason this is flagged rather than treated as one question: House prefixes every option with \"**Topical**\"; House carries a **fifth** option, \"None of the above\", that Grade Gain does not; and where House prints \"alpha **adrenergics**\" Grade Gain prints \"alpha adrenergic **BLOCKERS**\", which is a different drug from `L11`'s alpha **agonists** and makes Grade Gain's own option defective. The option order also differs. **No fold decision is made here** — the hub decides, and if it folds, this is an `alsoIn` rather than a second entry.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Prostaglandin analogues, miotics and alpha agonists increase aqueous drainage; beta blockers appear only in the deck's production-reducing box, which makes them the exception.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.78-79 (Ch.11 Q23; option D prints "prostaglandin analgoues". SEAM: options A-C close p.78, options D and E and the key open p.79)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following fibres are the most resistant to glaucomatous damage?",
    options:["Nasal fibres","Arcuate fibres","Papillomacular bundle","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The mirror of `opmcq-c11-15` — same five options, same order, one word changed, and the key moves from B to C.** The bundle anatomy, the measurement showing that **`papillomacular` returns zero hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters**, and the reason the module's own field sequence still settles the answer are all written in `opmcq-c11-15` and are not repeated.\n\n**The one line that decides this question is `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` line 157:** under *Visual acuity changes*, \"**Diminution of central vision in END STAGE disease**.\" Central vision is carried by the **papillomacular bundle**, so a disease that spares central acuity until the very end is a disease that spares that bundle until the very end. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` says the same thing from the field side: the defects are \"scotomas of various shapes and a contraction of the peripheral field that usually **spares the central vision until late** in the disease process\", and warns that \"much peripheral vision can be lost before the patient notices visual impairment\".\n\n**That sparing is the whole clinical problem with glaucoma.** A patient reading 6/6 can already have lost most of the field, which is why `L35,36` insists that screening is by **disc and field**, never by acuity, and why `opqb-t11-492` keys regular **field plotting** as the most reliable follow-up test.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Nasal fibres** | Damaged in the middle of the sequence — their loss is the **nasal step**, third on `L11`'s six-item list |\n| **B. Arcuate fibres** | The **most vulnerable**, and the key to `opmcq-c11-15` |\n| **C. Papillomacular bundle** | The key — the last to go, which is why acuity survives to end stage |\n| **D. All of the above** | Would deny the ordering the field sequence is built on |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails, C being correct |\n\n*⚠️ The anatomical reason the papillomacular fibres last longest — they enter the **temporal** disc, where the lamina cribrosa's plates are densest and its pores smallest, away from the superior and inferior poles that notch first — is **not taken from the course material**.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The papillomacular bundle is the last to be lost in glaucoma, which is why central acuity is spared until end-stage disease and why glaucoma is screened by disc and field rather than by vision.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.79 (Ch.11 Q24; identical option menu to Ch.11 Q15 on p.77, different key)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 60-year-old man with glaucoma does a visual field test. The printout shows a curved area of vision loss that arches above fixation in the pattern of the nerve fiber layer. What is this defect called?",
    options:["Central scotoma","Arcuate scotoma","Altitudinal defect","Ring scotoma"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"*⚠️ No perimetry printout is printed with this question. The bank describes the field in prose; the stem above is transcribed exactly as it stands.*\n\n**The stem gives the definition and asks for the name.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` line 151 lists \"**Arcuate scotomata (superior, inferior)**\" second in the glaucomatous field sequence, immediately after the paracentral scotomata of Bjerrum's area, and the deck's summary diagram prints \"Bjerrum scotoma · **Arcuate scotoma** · Nasal step · Ring scotoma\". *Arcuate* means arched, and the arch is the point: the defect follows the sweep of the retinal nerve fibre bundles as they curve around the macula to reach the disc above and below. A defect that \"arches **above** fixation\" is the superior arcuate scotoma, from loss of the **inferior** rim — the same inversion that makes `opmcq-c11-2`'s 6-to-7-o'clock notch produce a superior defect.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Central scotoma** | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.4 defines it as \"Seeing dark spots within the visual field... **affecting central vision or fixation**\". This defect arches **above** fixation and leaves it intact, and `L11` line 157 makes central loss the **end stage** of glaucoma, not its presenting field |\n| **B. Arcuate scotoma** | The key |\n| **C. Altitudinal defect** | Loss of a whole **half** of the field along the **horizontal** meridian, from an ischaemic optic neuropathy or a branch retinal artery occlusion rather than from glaucoma. It is not on `L11`'s six-item list at all, and Grade Gain uses it as the exception in the same way — `opqb-t11-468` keys \"**Altitudinal** field defect\" as the one that is NOT a characteristic POAG change |\n| **D. Ring scotoma** | On `L11`'s list, but later and larger: line 153 defines it as a \"**double arcuate** scotoma\" — the superior and inferior arcs joined into a ring. A single arc arching above fixation is one half of it |\n\n*The full six-item sequence — paracentral, arcuate, nasal step, ring, tubular field, temporal island — is written in `op-glauc`'s section on functional change; `opmcq-c11-4` is the recall version of this same question and `opmcq-c11-13` is the symptom version.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"An arc of field loss following the curve of the nerve fibre bundles is the arcuate scotoma, second in the glaucomatous field sequence; two of them joined make a ring scotoma, and an altitudinal defect is not glaucomatous at all.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.79 (Ch.11 Q25; the stem prints the American "fiber" where Ch.11 Q15 and Q24 print "fibres")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c11-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which class of medications lowers intraocular pressure by reducing aqueous humor production and is commonly used in the treatment of glaucoma?",
    options:["Prostaglandin analogues","Beta agonists","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Miotics"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The same two-box sort as `opmcq-c11-23`, read from the other side.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lines 209-212:\n\n> • **Decreasing aqueous humour production** → beta-blockers, **carbonic anhydrase inhibitors**, alpha agonists\n> • **Increasing aqueous humour drainage** → prostaglandin analogues, miotics, alpha agonists\n\nOf the four options offered, **only carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are in the production box** — and unlike alpha agonists, they are in that box alone. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lines 221-226 use them twice in the acute protocol, systemically as acetazolamide and topically as \"Topical CAIs\", and `opmcq-c11-9` keys them as the first line in an angle-closure crisis — twice over, since the bank printed that question twice for precisely this reason: cutting production is the only mechanism that still works when the drainage route is shut.\n\n⚠️ **Option B is printed as \"Beta AGONISTS\", not beta blockers, and the difference is the whole distractor.** `L11` line 210 puts beta-**blockers** in the production box; a beta **agonist** stimulates the ciliary epithelium and would if anything increase aqueous formation *(the direction is not taken from the course material — the module names beta-blockers only)*. A reader skimming for \"beta\" picks B and lands on the opposite drug.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Prostaglandin analogues** | Drainage box, `L11` line 212 — first in it |\n| **B. Beta agonists** | Not a glaucoma drug in this material; the printed class is beta-**blockers** |\n| **C. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors** | The key |\n| **D. Miotics** | Drainage box, `L11` line 212; `L12` line 226 names pilocarpine |\n\n*Grade Gain asks the inverse — `opqb-t11-457`, \"Which of the following drugs does NOT decrease aqueous formation?\", keyed **prostaglandin analogues** against beta blockers, CAI and alpha adrenergic agonists. Same `L11` sort, opposite polarity, and two of the four options differ (House offers Beta **agonists** and Miotics; Grade Gain offers Beta **blockers** and alpha agonists), so the two are not one question.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower IOP by reducing aqueous production — the deck's production box also holds beta-BLOCKERS and alpha agonists, while prostaglandin analogues and miotics work on drainage.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.79 (Ch.11 Q26, the last question of the chapter; option B prints "Beta agonists")' }
